Ad-hoc, temporary print-based debugging Rust code without new type constraints

Suppose I have some Rust code like

fn foo<T>(x: T) {
  // Complicated function.
}

fn function_that_calls_foo_to_honor_the_request_in_comments<T>(x: T) {
   foo(x)
}

that is called from a gazillion places all over my codebase.

Suppose, then, that I realize that something is wrong deep within foo. Sometimes, I might want to do some quick print-based debugging within the function. That would entail printing Ts with e.g. Debug formatting. Somewhere in the actual program being debugged, there are concrete instances of foo<T>, say e.g. foo<i64>. Since i64 implements Debug, I can easily add debugging print statements to foo<T> that would work for that particular monomorphization. However, since foo<T> is completely generic in T as it stands, the typechecker naturally demands that I restrict T: Debug. That would be OK to do temporarily and locally for debugging, except foo<T> is used in a gazillion places all over the code, so now all those sites need to get T: Debug too, which would be a real hassle to add and then remove. In the example above, imagine there are 100 more functions like function_that_calls_foo_to_honor_the_request_in_comments<T> and having to add the T: Debug constraint to all of them, and then remove that again after the debugging session.

Question: Is there a quick and dirty way to, for temporary debugging purposes only, add print statements to foo<T> that essentially say "if you're in a monomorphized version where T: Debug, perform these prints involving some Ts, otherwise don't (you can even exit or crash, I don't care)"?

I know that Rust is not geared for run-time type introspection, and I certainly don't propose using such mechanisms in general – I'm merely asking for a crude escape hatch for temporary debugging. I can envision something where there's a runtime check of the type name of T, and if it's say "i64" there's some transmutes into an i64 that's printed. I guess that would work, but maybe there's something a bit tidier?

Generally: I suppose the general question is "is there a way to tell the Rust compiler to do something as if a trait were satisfied even when the constraints don't promise it, and just die in a fireball if runtime reality doesn't match up?"

Should I override unused methods

I have an abstract base class that has a method to get some data. Concrete classes
provide a query which this method uses to retrieve that data. The class looks like this:

public abstract class BaseClass
{
   public virtual Data GetData()
   {
      var query = CreateQuery();
      var data = ... // using the query to get the data
      return data;
   }

   protected abstract Query CreateQuery();
}

A concrete implementation of this base class looks like this:

public class ConcreteClass : BaseClass
{
    protected override Query CreateQuery()
    {
        var query = new Query();
        // ... 
        return query;
    }
}

Now I have another class which should inherit from the above concrete implementation. This class doesn't retrieve the data via a query but creates it with some other mechanisms. Therefore it overrides the GetData method:

public class SpecializedClass : ConcreteClass
{
    public override Data GetData()
    {
        var data = /* Create the data */
        return data;
    } 
}

Should SpecializedClass override CreateQuery as well even if CreateQuery doesn't get used here? My example is in C# as that's the language I use but language agnostic answers are welcome.

How to test Widgets that display modal dialogs with QTest

In our project there are many widgets that use pop-up modal dialogs like QMessageBox::warning for user feedback whenever some operation fails. I would like to test them with QTest, but I can't find any good way to wait for them to appear.

For complete copy-pastable code scroll to the bottom of the question.

First (void test1()) I hoped that I could simulate QTest::mouseClick(failButton, Qt::LeftButton); and then use QTest::qWaitFor to wait for the modal to be instantiated. It unfortunately doesn't work, because QMessageBox modal appears immediately whenever function that creates it is called, or a signal connected to corresponding slot is emitted (even without event loop running). Then it blocks the test indefinitely. Here is the output of my first test, I had to click on the modal manually to unblock the test:

[     0.000] - Foo
[     0.005] - before click
[     0.005] - button clicked
[     2.608] - after click
[     3.609] - test1:  false

Then after reading this answer I tried the approach with QTimer and browsing through QApplication::topLevelWidgets(). It kind of works, but seems very error-prone, because the QTimer executes my logic just once after arbitrarily chosen interval. See void test2() and void test3() for scenario with modal called directly in the constructor. The output of 2 other tests is:

[     3.609] - Foo
[     3.610] - before click
[     3.610] - button clicked
[     4.612] - found message box with message:  "sample warning from button"
[     4.612] - after click
[     4.612] - test2:  true
[     4.612] - before Foo()
[     4.612] - Foo
[     5.614] - found message box with message:  "sample warning"
[     5.616] - after Foo()
[     5.616] - test3:  true

You can see that each time 1s is wasted because of one shot timer. Sure, I could make it 1ms instead, but what guarantee can I have that the main thread reaches modal creation before the timer fires?

How come the QTimer one shot works even without Qt event loop running?

What is the canonical way to assert that Widget displays a modal in modern Qt6?

Example code (I tried to godbolt it, but there are some linking issues):

#include <chrono>

#include <QApplication>
#include <QDebug>
#include <QMessageBox>
#include <QPushButton>
#include <QString>
#include <QTest>
#include <QTimer>

using namespace std::chrono_literals;

class Foo : public QWidget {
    Q_OBJECT
public:
    Foo(bool fail) {
        qDebug() << "Foo";
        if (fail) {
            QMessageBox::warning(this, "failure", "sample warning");
        }
        auto* pb = new QPushButton("fail", this);
        pb->setObjectName("failButton");
        QObject::connect(pb, &QPushButton::clicked, [this]() {
            qDebug() << "button clicked";
            QMessageBox::warning(this, "failure", "sample warning from button");
        });
    }
    ~Foo() override = default;
};

bool test1() {
    Foo sut{false};
    auto* failButton = sut.findChild<QPushButton*>();
    if (!failButton) {
        qDebug() << "failed to find failButton child";
        return false;
    }
    qDebug() << "before click";
    QTest::mouseClick(failButton, Qt::LeftButton);
    qDebug() << "after click";
    bool result = QTest::qWaitFor(
        []() {
            QWidgetList allToplevelWidgets = QApplication::topLevelWidgets();
            for (auto* w : allToplevelWidgets) {
                if (w->inherits("QMessageBox")) {
                    auto* mb = qobject_cast<QMessageBox*>(w);
                    qDebug() << "found message box with message: " << mb->text();
                    QTest::keyClick(mb, Qt::Key_Enter);
                    return true;
                }
            }
            return false;
        },
        QDeadlineTimer(1s));
    return result;
}

bool test2() {
    Foo sut{false};
    auto* failButton = sut.findChild<QPushButton*>();
    if (!failButton) {
        qDebug() << "failed to find failButton child";
        return false;
    }
    bool result{false};
    QTimer::singleShot(1s, [&result]() {
        QWidgetList allToplevelWidgets = QApplication::topLevelWidgets();
        for (auto* w : allToplevelWidgets) {
            if (w->inherits("QMessageBox")) {
                auto* mb = qobject_cast<QMessageBox*>(w);
                qDebug() << "found message box with message: " << mb->text();
                QTest::keyClick(mb, Qt::Key_Enter);
                result = true;
            }
        }
    });
    qDebug() << "before click";
    QTest::mouseClick(failButton, Qt::LeftButton);
    qDebug() << "after click";
    return QTest::qWaitFor([&result]() { return result; }, QDeadlineTimer(1s));
}

bool test3() {
    bool result{false};
    QTimer::singleShot(1s, [&result]() {
        QWidgetList allToplevelWidgets = QApplication::topLevelWidgets();
        for (auto* w : allToplevelWidgets) {
            if (w->inherits("QMessageBox")) {
                auto* mb = qobject_cast<QMessageBox*>(w);
                qDebug() << "found message box with message: " << mb->text();
                QTest::keyClick(mb, Qt::Key_Enter);
                result = true;
            }
        }
    });
    qDebug() << "before Foo()";
    Foo sut{true};
    qDebug() << "after Foo()";
    return QTest::qWaitFor([&result]() { return result; }, QDeadlineTimer(1s));
}

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    QApplication app(argc, argv);
    qDebug() << "test1: " << test1();
    qDebug() << "test2: " << test2();
    qDebug() << "test3: " << test3();
    return 0;
}

