How to safely manipulate or inspect stack frames in C++ using inline assembly without triggering Undefined Behavior?

I am writing a low-level routine in C++ that mixes inline assembly (or an external .s file) to inspect or modify the current stack frame.

Here is an example:

#include <iostream>

extern "C" void custom_stack_inspect();

void target_function() {
    int x = 42;
    custom_stack_inspect();
}

int main() {
    target_function();
    return 0;
}

And the assembly:

.global custom_stack_inspect
custom_stack_inspect:
    # What is the portable or ABI-compliant way to peek at caller's local 'x' here?
    ret

In x86_64 (System V vs. MSVC ABI), how does the compiler guarantee where local variables like x live relative to rbp or rsp, especially when optimizations (-O2 / -O3) eliminate the base pointer?

What exact C++ rules or Undefined Behavior clauses are violated if an assembly routine manually walks up the stack frame chain?

Why does increasing local size improve performance in OpenCL?

My 1D int array represents a texture where each value is a pixel. OpenCL draws rectangles with coordinates and colors on that texture, passed from the host.

I decided to set global size to the 1D array (texture) size, and local size to 1 (as well as dimension) when calling clEnqueueNDRangeKernel. The OpenCL kernel (that represents pixels) checks if the current pixel is in the rectangle, for each rectangle. If so it draws the color. If the host passes 100 rectangles for a 1,000 x 1,000 texture there will be 1,000,000 parallel threads, each of which loops through 100 rectangles.

host.cpp:

//startup setup

size_t textureSize = textureWidth * textureHeight * 4;
size_t textureSizeCL = sizeof(int) * textureSize;

cl_mem textureCL = clCreateBuffer(context, CL_MEM_READ_WRITE, textureSizeCL, NULL, &err);

int *texture = new int[textureSize];

...

cl_mem commandsCL = clCreateBuffer(context, CL_MEM_READ_ONLY, COMMANDS_BUFFER_SIZE_CL, NULL, &err);
int *commands = new int[COMMANDS_BUFFER_SIZE];

...

clSetKernelArg(kernel, 0, sizeof(cl_mem), &textureCL);
clSetKernelArg(kernel, 1, sizeof(cl_mem), &commandsCL);
clSetKernelArg(kernel, 2, sizeof(cl_int), &textureWidth);
clSetKernelArg(kernel, 3, sizeof(cl_int), &textureHeight);

...

// loop

size_t global = textureSize;
size_t local = 1;

clEnqueueNDRangeKernel(commands, kernel, 1, NULL, &global, &local, 0, NULL, NULL);

kernel.cl:

__kernel void shader(__global int* texture, __constant int* commands, int width, int height)
{
    int id = get_global_id(0); // from 0 to width*height

    // current pixel coordinates
    int y = id/width;
    int x = id - y * width;

    int rectLeft, rectTop, rectRight, rectBottom, rectColor;

    for(int i = 0; commands[i] != STOP_CODE; i++)
    {
        if(commands[i] != NEXT_RECTANGLE)
        {
            // read rectLeft, rectTop, ...
        }
        else if(x > left && x < right && y > top && y < bottom)
        {
            texture[id] = rectColor;
        }
    }
}

It was slow. Then, without changing the kernel on the host side, I set local size to 100. 1,000 x 1,000 global work items were grouped to 10,000 local groups with 100 work items in each.

How did that make it run faster without refactoring the kernel?

Why does removing an entry from a HashMap during iteration throw ConcurrentModificationException?

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> scores = new HashMap<>();
        scores.put("Alice", 80);
        scores.put("Bob", 40);
        scores.put("Charlie", 90);

        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : scores.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() < 50) {
                scores.remove(entry.getKey());
            }
        }

        System.out.println(scores);
    }
}

When I run this code, it can throw a ConcurrentModificationException during iteration.

I understand that modifying a collection while iterating over it can be problematic, but I would like to understand the underlying behavior more precisely.

My questions are:

Why does modifying the map directly cause this exception?

Why is removing an entry through the iterator's remove() method treated differently?

Is using Iterator.remove() the recommended approach here, or is there a better alternative in modern Java?

I am using Java 21.

I would appreciate an explanation of the iterator behavior and the recommended approach for this situation.

Content Negotiation Standard HTTP response

For an e-commerce ERP. Should I leave generic http response messages to the public routes such as the cart checkout; or be descriptive to the client?
The user doesn't have a logging account.
The app uses flask.
The alternative is a customized response per public route with responses in jsonify in 400, 401, 402, 404, 405, and 500;
for instance;

if variant.stock_quantity < order_item.quantity:
    db.session.rollback()
        return {"error": "Insufficient stock"}, 409;

or;

if variant.stock_quantity < order_item.quantity:
    db.session.rollback()
    # let centralized error handling respond
        abort(409)
